.TH UBERTOOTH\-EGO 1 "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
.PP
.BR ubertooth-ego (1) 
\- Yuneec E\-Go sniffing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubertooth\-ego \-f
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The Yuneec E\-Go is an electric skateboard controlled by a wireless
handheld remote. This handheld remote uses a custom protocol implemented
in 2.4 GHz RF. 
.BR ubertooth-ego (1) 
can sniff this protocol and interfere
with its operation.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Sniff the connection between an E\-Go and its remote:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubertooth\-ego \-f
.fi
.RE
.PP
Receive all E\-Go packets on 2408 MHz:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubertooth\-ego \-r \-c 2408
.fi
.RE
.PP
Interfere with a connection:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubertooth\-ego \-i
.fi
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
Major modes:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-f\fR :
Sniff a connection by hopping along channels
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-r\fR :
Continuously receive packets on a single channel (see \fB\fC\-c\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-i\fR :
Interfere with a connection
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-j\fR :
Interfere with a connection (alias of \fB\fC\-i\fR)
.RE
.PP
Options:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-c <2402\-2480>\fR :
Channel in MHz to use for continuous receive (suggested: 2408, 2418,
2423, 2469)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-l <1\-48>\fR :
Packet length to use. The value must be a complete decimal integer from 1
through 48; invalid values fail before the Ubertooth is opened.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-a <access_code>\fR :
Access code to use, given as 2, 4, 6 or 8 hex digits (1 to 4 whole
bytes, default: 630f9ffe)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-U <index|serial>\fR :
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see
.BR ubertooth (7),
SELECTING A DEVICE
.RE
.SH TRANSMIT POWER
.PP
Interference (\fB\fC\-i\fR, \fB\fC\-j\fR) transmits at the CC2400 power level set with
\fB\fCubertooth\-util \-a\fR and leaves it unchanged\. A board that has not been given a
level jams at 7 (0 dBm, the maximum), the CC2400's reset value and the level
jamming always used before\. Sniffing does not transmit and does not change the
level\. These levels are at the CC2400 radio; the Ubertooth One's RF amplifier
changes the actual output after that\.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.PP
\fB\fCubertooth\-ego\fR takes options only. A word that is not an option, including
a bare \fB\fC\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named:
\fB\fCerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1.
.PP
\fB\fC\-h\fR ends the command only once the whole command line has
parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even
alongside it.
.PP
An option value must be a whole value in its range, or it is refused the same
way instead of being read as 0, for example
\fB\fCerror: channel must be 2402\-2480 MHz (got 2481)\fR\&.
